    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Major climate regimes of the Pacific Ocean such as ENSO affect physical and biological variables. Thermocline structure, current structure, and zooplankton density are examined in April and/or May of 2003, 2004, and 2005 under different ENSO climate conditions using ADCP, CTD, and neuston net tows. The thermocline depth in May 2003, a year with a lower Oceanic Nino Index, is higher between 0ºN and 5ºN and lower between 5ºN and 10ºN than April 2004 and May 2005 – both months with greater Oceanic Nino Indexes. There is greater eastward current between 10ºN and 15ºN in April 2004 and May 2005 than in May 2003. Zooplankton densities did not show significant differences between the three sampling periods. Long term studies of such physical and biological variables are necessary to understand the impacts of climate.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
